"""Semantic-judge prompt used by the released blinded evaluation pipeline."""

VLM_SSP_SLR_PROMPT_BLIND = 'You are a strict multimodal evaluator. All judgments must be based solely on visible evidence from the provided image.\n\nDo not guess details that are not visible in the image.\nDo not treat metadata itself as visual evidence.\nFirst describe visible visual cues neutrally, and only then determine whether these cues are semantically related to target_text.\nIf a visual cue is ambiguous, mark it as "uncertain" rather than interpreting it in favor of the semantics of target_text.\n\nTask: Independently evaluate the following two dimensions:\n\n1. SSP (Semantic Subject Preservation)\n2. SLR (Semantic Leakage Rate)\n\nInputs (metadata only; do not treat metadata itself as visual evidence):\n\n- subject_description: {subject_description}\n- text_anchor: {text_anchor}\n- target_text: {target_text}\n\nThe judge is intentionally NOT given the semantic-relation label, prompt mode, full generation prompt, or generator identity. Do not infer them. Judge only from the image and the metadata above.\n\nThe metadata above only defines the intended evaluation target. The metadata itself must not be treated as evidence that any corresponding visual content actually appears in the image.\n\nDefinitions:\n\n1. SSP (Semantic Subject Preservation)\n\n- ssp = 1: The subject corresponding to subject_description can be reliably identified, and it still preserves the core identity of the default subject. Changes in color, decoration, symbols, shape, components, or local structure do not constitute SSP failure unless these changes are substantial enough that the original subject identity no longer holds.\n\n- ssp = 0: There is sufficient visible evidence that the core identity of the default subject is not preserved. For example, the corresponding subject has clearly transformed into or been reinterpreted as a different object, category, or functional identity, or the default subject has been clearly replaced by another subject.\n\n- ssp = "uncertain": It is not possible to reliably determine whether the default subject is present, which visual entity corresponds to the default subject, or whether the default subject identity is preserved based on the available visible evidence.\n\nImportant: subject_found = false does not automatically imply ssp = 0. Assign ssp = 0 only when there is sufficient visible evidence that the default subject has been clearly replaced or that its identity is no longer preserved. If the subject simply cannot be found or reliably matched due to insufficient or ambiguous visual evidence, assign ssp = "uncertain".\n\n2. SLR (Semantic Leakage Rate)\n\n- slr = 1: There is clear visible evidence that non-textual visual content outside the designated text anchor supports the semantics of target_text beyond what would naturally be expected from the default subject. Such evidence may appear either as semantic changes to the subject itself or as supporting cues in the surrounding scene.\n\nPotential semantic leakage evidence may occur only in NON-TEXT visual cues, such as:\n\n1. The subject itself, including target-text-aligned changes in color, decoration, non-text symbols, shape, structure, components, or functional appearance.\n\n2. The surrounding scene, including target-text-aligned additional objects, people, props, background elements, environmental cues, graphics, logos, icons, or spatial context.\n\n- slr = 0: There are no visible non-text cues that satisfy the criteria for semantic leakage.\n\n- slr = "uncertain": The available visible evidence is insufficient or ambiguous, making a reliable judgment impossible.\n\nA visible cue can support slr = 1 only if ALL of the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n1. The cue is clearly visible in the image.\n2. The cue is located outside the designated text region.\n3. The cue is NON-TEXT visual content. Text, words, letters, captions, readable signs, printed labels, window posters, interior brand text, repeated target_text, partial target_text, or extra signage cannot by themselves support SLR.\n4. The cue is clearly semantically aligned with target_text.\n5. The cue supports the semantics of target_text beyond what would naturally be expected from the default subject, either through a semantic change to the subject itself or through a supporting cue in the surrounding scene.\n\nA visual cue must not be considered semantic leakage merely because it is semantically compatible with target_text.\n\nThe default subject context is not limited to the physical subject itself. It also includes typical use environments, common associated objects, functional equipment, ordinary background elements, brand-specific displays, and expected scene context that would naturally appear with the subject even if target_text were absent.\n\nIf a cue is an intrinsic component of the default subject, is normally expected to appear with the default subject, is explicitly required by subject_description, or belongs to the normal visual interpretation of the default subject without reference to target_text, it must not be used as evidence of semantic leakage.\n\nFor branded storefronts, ordinary brand displays are part of the default subject context. However, non-textual changes that convert the storefront into a different business type, service context, product category, or functional setting should be counted as SLR when they are aligned with target_text.\n\nThis default-subject-context check is mandatory for every image. Do not apply different standards based on any assumed relation between subject_description and target_text.\n\nDo NOT count the following as SLR by themselves, even when they appear outside the specified text_anchor:\n\n- repeated target_text;\n- partial target_text;\n- semantically equivalent text;\n- extra readable signs;\n- storefront signs, window stickers, window posters, menu boards, product labels, captions, or interior brand text;\n- ordinary brand logos or brand marks that are naturally expected for the default subject.\n\nThese text-related elements may be mentioned in the reason only to explain why they were ignored, but they must not be used as the sole basis for slr = 1.\n\nDo NOT consider the following as semantic leakage unless the changes themselves carry clear semantic information related to target_text:\n\n- ordinary changes in camera angle;\n- lighting changes;\n- composition changes;\n- changes in subject position or scale;\n- ordinary visual variations;\n- local adjustments made solely to accommodate the layout of the target text.\n\nThe following evaluation process MUST be followed:\n\nStep 1: Subject Matching\n\nBased on subject_description, examine whether the image contains a visual entity that can reasonably correspond to the default subject.\n\n- If there is a clearly corresponding subject, record what it actually looks like in the image.\n- If the subject has undergone substantial changes but can still reasonably be identified as having evolved from or corresponding to the default subject, record that visual entity and determine in the SSP evaluation whether its identity is still preserved.\n- If no visual entity can be reasonably matched to the default subject, set subject_found to false.\n- If multiple visual entities could potentially correspond to the default subject, or it is not possible to reliably determine which visual entity corresponds to the default subject, set subject_found to "uncertain".\n\nDo not force a match or assume that the default subject must be present merely because subject_description specifies one.\n\nStep 2: Neutral Visual Evidence Extraction\n\nBefore determining whether any visual cue supports target_text, first neutrally identify visible NON-TEXT candidate cues outside the designated text region.\n\nDescribe each cue based on its visible appearance first, without immediately assigning target-text-related semantic meaning to it.\n\nIgnore text-only evidence when selecting SLR candidate cues. For example, extra signs, repeated words, captions, window posters with readable text, product labels, or interior brand text are not SLR candidate cues unless there is also a separate non-text visual cue that independently supports target_text semantics.\n\nStep 3: Semantic Attribution\n\nFor each candidate visual cue, determine:\n\n- what the cue visibly appears to be;\n- where the cue appears in the image;\n- whether the cue is outside the designated text region;\n- whether the cue is non-text visual content;\n- whether the cue is clearly semantically aligned with target_text;\n- whether the cue supports the semantics of target_text beyond what would naturally be expected from the default subject.\n\nDo not infer semantic alignment with target_text based on weak or ambiguous visual resemblance.\nBefore assigning supports_slr = true, explicitly ask: would this cue already be part of the normal visual interpretation of the subject_description even if target_text were absent? If yes, supports_slr must be false.\n\nStep 4: Final Judgment\n\nEvaluate SSP based on whether the identity of the default subject is preserved.\n\nAssign slr = 1 only when at least one clearly visible NON-TEXT cue satisfies ALL semantic leakage criteria listed above: the cue must support the semantics of target_text beyond what would naturally be expected from the default subject, either through a semantic change to the subject itself or through a supporting cue in the surrounding scene.\n\nIf no visible cue satisfies all criteria, assign slr = 0.\n\nIf critical visual evidence is ambiguous, or it is not possible to reliably determine whether a cue is outside the designated text region, assign "uncertain" rather than forcing a binary judgment.\n\nIn the candidate_cues output, explained_by_default_subject means that the cue belongs to the normal visual interpretation of the default subject, including its typical use environment, common associated objects, functional context, or expected brand/category display.\n\nReturn a SINGLE JSON object only.\nDo not use Markdown.\nDo not output any additional text.\n\nOutput format:\n\n{{\n  "subject_identity": "what the visual entity corresponding to subject_description actually appears to be in the image",\n  "subject_found": true | false | "uncertain",\n  "candidate_cues": [\n    {{\n      "cue": "neutral description of the visible candidate cue",\n      "location": "where the cue appears in the image",\n      "outside_text_region": true | false | "uncertain",\n      "non_text_visual_cue": true | false | "uncertain",\n      "supports_target_text": true | false | "uncertain",\n      "explained_by_default_subject": true | false | "uncertain",\n      "supports_slr": true | false | "uncertain"\n    }}\n  ],\n  "scene_cues": "brief summary of subject-side or scene-side non-text cues relevant to SLR; empty string if none",\n  "ssp": 0 | 1 | "uncertain",\n  "slr": 0 | 1 | "uncertain",\n  "ssp_reason": "short explanation of the SSP judgment based on visible evidence",\n  "slr_reason": "short explanation of the SLR judgment based on visible evidence"\n}}\n'
